THOUGHT: Now I understand the root cause clearly. Let me analyze the vulnerable code in `TT_Process_Simple_Glyph`:

The vulnerable code:
```c
#ifdef TT_CONFIG_OPTION_GX_VAR_SUPPORT
    if ( FT_IS_NAMED_INSTANCE( FT_FACE( loader->face ) ) ||
         FT_IS_VARIATION( FT_FACE( loader->face ) )      )
    {
      unrounded = gloader->current.extra_points2;
      error = TT_Vary_Apply_Glyph_Deltas( ... );

      if ( !( loader->face->variation_support & TT_FACE_FLAG_VAR_HADVANCE ) )
        loader->linear = FT_PIX_ROUND( unrounded[n_points - 3].x -
                                       unrounded[n_points - 4].x ) / 64;
      if ( !( loader->face->variation_support & TT_FACE_FLAG_VAR_VADVANCE ) )
        loader->vadvance = FT_PIX_ROUND( unrounded[n_points - 1].x -
                                         unrounded[n_points - 2].x ) / 64;

      if ( error )
        return error;
    }
#endif
```

The bug: `unrounded` points to `gloader->current.extra_points2`. This array is NOT initialized. `TT_Vary_Apply_Glyph_Deltas` fills `unrounded` only if it succeeds. But if `TT_Vary_Apply_Glyph_Deltas` returns an error (e.g., invalid gvar table), the `unrounded` array is not filled, and then the code reads `unrounded[n_points-3]`, `unrounded[n_points-4]`, etc. BEFORE checking `if ( error ) return error;`. This reads uninitialized memory.

